  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...A strong cold front brings gusty winds, potential \nfor severe weather, and below average temperatures.\n\nModel guidance is in relatively good agreement showing a potent, \npositively-tilted trough stretching from the Great Lakes down \ntowards the Great Plains tracking eastward this morning. At the \nsurface, the trough pushes a strong cold front across the \nforecast area this afternoon and into the evening. This morning,\nahead of the front, cloud cover decreases allowing for sunny \nconditions and warm temperatures. Highs today rise into the 80s \nfor most with higher elevations staying in the 70s. With warm \nconditions and sunny skies, a line of thunderstorms is expected \nto develop along the passing cold front. The Storm Prediction \nCenter has much of the area east of the Blue Ridge/Catoctins and\nnorth of US-50/I-66 in a Slight Risk (level 2 out of 5) for \nsevere weather, driven primarily by a risk of damaging wind \ngusts. The highest probabilities are in the vicinity of NE MD \n(30% probs). Additionally, a quick QLCS tornado is possible, \nmainly in NE MD, where SPC also has a 2% tornado risk.\n\nPrecipitation lingers behind the cold front as the upper trough \npivots overhead tonight. Gusty winds remain possible beginning \nthis evening and persisting through Saturday. Winds will likely\nremain elevated across most of the area, although the higher \nelevations along the Alleghenies may have the highest peak \ngusts (between 30 and 40 mph). All areas are expected to remain\nbelow Wind Advisory criteria, although a few isolated gusts \nto near 45 mph are possible along the highest ridges.\n\nNoticeably cooler temperatures rush in behind the front with \nhigh temperatures on Saturday being approximately 25-30 degrees \ncolder compared to today. Highs on Saturday will be in the 40s \nand 50s across the forecast area with overnight lows dropping \ninto the 30s to low 40s. Frost/Freeze headlines are likely for \nearly Sunday morning, mainly west of I-95 where winds will be \nlower.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Dry conditions through next week with temperatures \nreturning to mid-October normals.\n\nHeading into next week, high pressure remains overhead through the \nmiddle of the week. A dry and benign weather pattern is expected\nduring this time with near normal temperatures for mid-October.\nAlthough several global ensemble members depict a coastal low \nforming off the Mid-Atlantic shore early next week, most members\nmaintain dry and seasonable weather. If this low pressure \nsystem were to move closer to the coast, chances for \nprecipitation could change next week, but confidence is low.",
